1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,309
A dashboard should feel like a live instrument.

2
00:00:03,429 --> 00:00:09,373
A new observation arrives, the right number changes, and the person using it keeps their
place.

3
00:00:09,493 --> 00:00:18,131
Foldlite D B gives you the building blocks for that experience: immutable documents,
incrementally maintained views, and cursor based updates.

4
00:00:18,251 --> 00:00:22,918
Let's look at the engineering underneath, starting with a quick map reduce refresher.

5
00:00:24,488 --> 00:00:27,472
Think of each document as an observation you keep.

6
00:00:27,592 --> 00:00:35,661
The native Rust engine validates a batch, computes its view changes, then appends and
syncs the complete batch before acknowledging it.

7
00:00:35,781 --> 00:00:37,139
Documents are immutable.

8
00:00:37,259 --> 00:00:41,009
Indexes, reduced state, and change history are derived.

9
00:00:41,129 --> 00:00:48,327
That separation is useful: the facts outlive any particular interpretation, and a broken
cache can be reconstructed.

10
00:00:49,897 --> 00:00:54,890
Map turns a document into zero or more key and value contributions.

11
00:00:55,010 --> 00:01:01,790
Here, three latency observations emit the same key, with values forty two, twelve, and
six.

12
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:05,033
The host groups those contributions by key.

13
00:01:05,153 --> 00:01:12,490
Reduce folds them into explicit state: count three, sum sixty, minimum six, maximum forty
two.

14
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:17,161
Finalize derives the public result, including a mean of twenty.

15
00:01:17,281 --> 00:01:19,046
That's the bundled metrics view.

16
00:01:20,616 --> 00:01:24,111
Now another latency observation arrives: eighteen.

17
00:01:24,231 --> 00:01:30,349
The engine maps the new document and adds its contribution to the existing latency state.

18
00:01:30,469 --> 00:01:35,821
Count becomes four, sum becomes seventy eight, and the mean becomes nineteen point five.

19
00:01:35,941 --> 00:01:38,670
The unrelated checkout group keeps its result.

20
00:01:38,790 --> 00:01:45,500
You're maintaining an answer as observations arrive, without replaying all previous
observations for every update.

21
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:51,796
For a native view consumer, start with a snapshot and keep its cursor.

22
00:01:51,916 --> 00:01:57,686
Drain all snapshot pages against that same snapshot, then ask for changes after the
cursor.

23
00:01:57,806 --> 00:02:01,010
Change rows contain complete resulting key states.

24
00:02:01,130 --> 00:02:08,723
Replace each affected key in your application state, drain the change pages, and save the
resulting state together with its cursor.

25
00:02:08,843 --> 00:02:13,998
Your application chooses the network transport; a cursor alone is not a subscription.

26
00:02:15,568 --> 00:02:19,701
Here's a working example with a different placement of the same computation.

27
00:02:19,821 --> 00:02:23,536
A local application streams documents over server sent events.

28
00:02:23,656 --> 00:02:31,841
The browser ingests them into Foldlite's worker, runs the actual metrics WebAssembly
module, and applies changed rows by key.

29
00:02:31,961 --> 00:02:35,189
Watch latency update while checkout stays put.

30
00:02:35,309 --> 00:02:39,233
Pause the connection, let more facts arrive, then reconnect.

31
00:02:39,353 --> 00:02:42,743
The browser catches up from its last applied document cursor.

32
00:02:44,313 --> 00:02:47,993
Reliable streaming needs an explicit recovery story.

33
00:02:48,113 --> 00:02:55,730
Register a subscriber before capturing its replay watermark, then replay the missing
prefix before following live updates.

34
00:02:55,850 --> 00:03:01,655
Foldlite's stream helper skips queued duplicates and uses bounded subscriber queues.

35
00:03:01,775 --> 00:03:03,946
Overflow produces a gap event.

36
00:03:04,066 --> 00:03:09,104
Reconnect from the last applied stream cursor, or bootstrap again when required.

37
00:03:09,224 --> 00:03:12,823
Keep document stream cursors separate from view cursors.

38
00:03:12,943 --> 00:03:18,354
After a native rebuild, expired view cursors require a fresh snapshot.

39
00:03:19,924 --> 00:03:22,710
The portable part is especially interesting.

40
00:03:22,830 --> 00:03:27,486
Write map and reduce in AssemblyScript and compile one WebAssembly module.

41
00:03:27,606 --> 00:03:32,145
Those exact bytes run inside the native Rust engine and a browser worker.

42
00:03:32,265 --> 00:03:36,421
Inputs and state are explicit, and the view must be deterministic.

43
00:03:36,541 --> 00:03:40,059
The worker keeps computation away from the page's main thread.

44
00:03:40,179 --> 00:03:45,845
The browser host owns in memory storage; it does not run the Rust durable log engine.

45
00:03:47,415 --> 00:03:49,749
A native checkpoint accelerates recovery.

46
00:03:49,869 --> 00:03:55,140
Its identity includes the exact module digest and a verified log prefix.

47
00:03:55,260 --> 00:04:01,738
Reopen verifies the log, loads a valid cache, and computes the uncached suffix.

48
00:04:01,858 --> 00:04:08,777
Lose the cache, or change the module bytes, and the engine rebuilds derived state from the
original batches.

49
00:04:08,897 --> 00:04:14,459
Preserve the document log and exact view artifact to reproduce an interpretation.

50
00:04:14,579 --> 00:04:19,281
Stable document identities also make identical ingestion retries a no op.

51
00:04:20,851 --> 00:04:22,569
You can model more than metrics.

52
00:04:22,689 --> 00:04:31,338
The bundled catalogue view folds item events in ingestion order: add an item, rename it,
then remove it from the result with a deletion event.

53
00:04:31,458 --> 00:04:33,873
The source observations remain intact.

54
00:04:33,993 --> 00:04:37,581
This fold is order sensitive, so it is not mergeable.

55
00:04:37,701 --> 00:04:42,391
Implement merge only when independent states really can combine correctly.

56
00:04:42,511 --> 00:04:48,212
Timestamps do not reorder your events; the application chooses the domain order before
ingestion.

57
00:04:49,782 --> 00:04:51,813
The current design has clear limits.

58
00:04:51,933 --> 00:04:55,486
One native database holds one view and has one active owner.

59
00:04:55,606 --> 00:04:58,729
Host memory grows with documents, groups, and history.

60
00:04:58,849 --> 00:05:02,785
Browser storage is in memory, so export before reload.

61
00:05:02,905 --> 00:05:07,746
There is no built in replication or automatic multi client synchronization.

62
00:05:07,866 --> 00:05:11,338
Your application owns the transport and bootstrap policy.

63
00:05:11,458 --> 00:05:18,424
Those boundaries help you judge where this fits, without assuming distributed system
features or unmeasured performance.

64
00:05:19,994 --> 00:05:25,799
The compelling idea is a short, inspectable path from an accepted fact to a changed pixel.

65
00:05:25,919 --> 00:05:31,898
Keep observations, maintain the view, apply updates by key, and make recovery deliberate.

66
00:05:32,018 --> 00:05:37,985
That is a strong foundation for telemetry dashboards, activity feeds, and evolving
catalogues.

67
00:05:38,105 --> 00:05:42,970
Explore the playground, read the design, or run the demo included with this video.
